WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.359
Audiences expecting to see the big-screen
collaboration between comedy king Judd Apatow

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:09.480
and Saturday Night Live's Pete Davidson got
a rude awakening after Universal pulled the

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.260
film just two days before it was set to release.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.380
With most theaters still closed nationwide
and few new releases available in the country's

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.760
handful of open indoor and drive-in establishments,
audiences are starved for new movie material.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.820
So it's no surprise that those who purchased
tickets for Thursday night and weekend screenings

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.420
of Davidson's semi-autobiographical dramedy
were angry when they discovered their tickets

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.200
had been refunded.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.610
News of the cancellations first came on the
Thursday before the movie's opening weekend,

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:40.560
when establishments like the Warwick Drive-In
in New York and the Rustic Tri View Drive-In

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.900
in Rhode Island began posting their refund
policies and announcing the nixed screenings.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.620
In response to faulty information circulating
online prior to the movie's release, Apatow

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.880
took to Twitter to try to clarify the situation.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.020
In an all-caps announcement, the Freaks and
Geeks and Pineapple Express producer tweeted,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.860
"THE KING OF STATEN ISLAND IS ONLY OPENING
ON VOD FRIDAY.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.300
IT IS NOT OPENING IN THEATERS."

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.799
In response to a Twitter user who pointed
out it was scheduled to appear at their local

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.040
theater, Apatow replied,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.120
"It won’t be playing there.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.300
It is a mistake.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.800
It is only on VOD."

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.460
So... what happened?

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.320
"Nobody told you about V-O-D?"

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.000
"...What is that?"

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.220
Variety reports that the screening cancellations
weren't the fault of theater owners, who were

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.520
also in the dark about the move.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.820
One anonymous independent theater owner even
told the outlet that there was not immediately

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.759
any explanation provided about the abrupt
reversal, and that the studio merely, quote,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.520
"changed their mind."

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.880
Insiders at the studio confirmed this to Variety,
revealing that the last-minute decision to

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.119
pull The King of Staten Island from theaters
was the result of a major mix-up linked to

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.020
Universal's controversial video-on-demand
release strategy.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.780
"You make everyone around you feel crazy."

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.500
Speaking on the basis of anonymity, Universal
studio insiders confirmed to Variety that

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.040
the company's original release intentions
were focused exclusively on a VOD release.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:01.380
It was an internal misunderstanding among
executives that led to the film wrongly being

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:07.090
sold to around 100 theaters nationwide before
studio leadership had to reach out to theater

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.520
owners and request they not screen the film.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:15.460
The move angered some, with one Twitter user
sarcastically tweeting, "Real class act, Universal,"

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.060
in response to Apatow's tweets.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.880
An unnamed screen operator shared that disgruntled
ticket-holders had passed along similar sentiments

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:23.380
to them.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.440
The source told Variety,

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.240
"This caused a considerable amount of ill
will with customers who bought tickets online

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.620
showing up for the Thursday 7 p.m. show."

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.420
That wasn't the only misstep Universal made
around the release of The King of Staten Island,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:36.920
either.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.260
Another anonymous theater owner revealed that
before Universal abruptly yanked one of its

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.220
latest films from playing on the big screen,
the studio negotiated questionable ticket

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.560
sales terms with theaters.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.440
Reports indicate that the studio pushed for
profit-sharing terms that split ticket earnings

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.280
evenly with the cinemas, something that's
traditionally only done with major releases.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.920
In a time when theaters are being hit particularly
hard due to industry-wide closures, some viewed

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.340
the ask as too demanding.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.080
Especially as the film, which would have mostly
screened at drive-ins, would have also been

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.960
available to rent at similar ticket costs
online.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.920
As one independent theater owner put it,

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.080
"They wanted 2019 terms in 2020 conditions.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.820
This is a new landscape."

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:23.660
The incident with The King of Staten Island
isn't the only thing Universal has done lately

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.060
that's made distributors angry.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:31.370
Following the VOD release of Trolls World
Tour, NBCUniversal CEO Jeff Shell implied

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.840
the studio was seriously considering on-demand
as a new distribution stream post-pandemic.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.380
The comment sparked a heated back-and-forth
in the press between the studio, the popular

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:45.520
cinema chain AMC Theatres, and the National
Association of Theatre Owners.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.540
At the time, Adam Aron, AMC Theatres' chair-CEO,
declared in a letter that the company would

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.220
"no longer play any Universal movies in any
of our theaters in the United States, Europe

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:02.040
or the Middle East" putting major tentpoles
like F9 in jeopardy after they were delayed

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.240
to ensure a proper theatrical release.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:10.200
Patrick Corcoran, vice president and CCO of
the National Association of Theatre Owners,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.320
also called the Comcast-owned studio out,
stating,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.930
"Universal has a destructive tendency to ... announce
decisions affecting their exhibitor partners

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.320
without actually consulting with those partners."

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:26.600
It was largely believed that things were still
tense between the three entities until Variety

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.389
reported that during a recent earnings call,
Aron attempted to quell the flames from their

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:32.000
public fight earlier this year.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.060
He told the outlet,

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.020
"Relations are warm with Universal.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.420
Relations with Universal have always been
warm.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.440
There is nothing personal about this issue
with Universal ... this is just an issue about

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:41.940
money."

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.199
While things may have warmed, the response
to Universal's latest move is looking more

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.440
like The King of Staten Island mishap may
re-ignite that fight.

